 "notes": "No PRF node: the tetrahedron's 'performance' (ns-scale transformation) is a kinetic property, not a use-condition claim; MatMech M3's 'ultrafast phase switching applications' is unsupported by text. Here the processing act (laser pulse, a8) is the phenomenon under study, not a fabrication step — hence PRC/stimulus/transient. The spine is dominated by OBS→OBS data reduction (pattern → radial profile → fraction → kinetic curve → TTT → fitted Ea), which seed rels cannot express; proposed rel reduces_to. Image-vs-text: (1) F4 difference-pattern panel shows a dashed diffuse 'Liquid Phase' hump at 1900 K that the text does not discuss — partial melting in the quantified snapshot. (2) F5: at 1575, 1450, 1400 and 1200 K the fitted curves show a shoulder/plateau at ~0.2–0.3 (two-stage kinetics), not a single S-shape as the text says. (3) F3: the ≥1600 K plateau looks ~5 µs rather than the 6.5 µs stated; 1200 K kinetics in F5 extend to ~10 µs, i.e. into the cooling regime of F3. (4) F7: frames are very noisy; the 'lens-shaped β grains' rest on a single annotated arrow; grain growth before/after is barely discernible — a21 fit=poor. (5) F14: CK model diverges steeply below 1400 K (off-scale), stronger than text implies. Argument branches at a15: one branch goes to empirical mechanism (Pati–Cohen fit), the other to first-principles model prediction (CK/GOO), which rejoin at model validation."
}